4.3. Collective comfort probabilities
Determining a proper strategy to enhance collective comfort in multi-occupancy environments has been a key question for building researchers [53]. Jung and Jazizadeh [54] listed the established methods for collective comfort improvements over the years as majority rule, error minimization, collective learning, and thermal-comfort-sensitivity-based optimization. Although proven to have potential in different cases in the literature, all these strategies were designated to find an optimal setpoint temperature, assuming the uniformity of the thermal conditions. In this study, we demonstrated that temperature is not uniformly distributed in large multi-occupancy
spaces and thermal conditions that occupants are subjected to vary based on the contextual factors in their immediate surroundings.
To this end, we concentrated on the temperature values at occupant locations rather than the room averages and analyzed the potential of coupling temperature distribution patterns and personalized comfort profiles. In doing so, we assigned a unique comfort profile to each of the six occupants illustrated in Section 3.1 and assessed collective comfort probabilities in different occupancy scenarios considering the related operative temperatures at the occupied locations. Table 4 presents the collective comfort probabilities at minimum multi-occupancy scenarios with two occupants. Accordingly, collective comfort probability at the baseline, where occupant positions are fixed, and supply airflow rate is constant with uniform distribution towards all directions, was calcu-lated as 68% on average. Introducing adjustability to the supply airflow direction at the diffuser level in control strategy-1 increased the prob-ability of achieving collective comfort to 79%, while allowing alter-ations in both rate and direction of supplied airflow in control strategy-2 resulted in 90% probability on average. In accordance with ASHRAE [7], a building zone is considered to satisfy comfort requirements if the comfortable occupants compile at least 80% of the room population. Translating this to the approach adopted in this study, achieving col-lective comfort with a probability of at least 80% could be claimed as the targeted level. Although control strategy-1 and control strategy-2 improved collective comfort probability, both failed to satisfy 80% comfort probability for all occupancy scenarios when considering the personalized thermal comfort profiles. This implies that, in some cases, occupants would be dissatisfied with the thermal conditions of the space regardless of the supply airflow settings. However, control strategy-3, which allows dynamic allocation of the occupants between six posi-tions together with supply airflow alterations, offered substantial im-provements in collective comfort probabilities and complied with the minimum comfort requirements in all scenarios. With a probability of 98% on average, utilizing control strategy-3 was shown to provide almost seamless operation for ensuring comfort in minimum multi-occupancy.
In Table 4, changes in supplied airflow rate were illustrated as changes in energy use, due to their correlation based on the cubic relationship between flow rate and fan power. Accordingly, control strategy-1 does not have any implications on energy use, in which the supplied airflow rate was fixed. Control strategy-2 offered energy sav-ings in nine out of fifteen cases, while improving the collective comfort probabilities. A superior energy performance was suggested by control strategy-3, with which the collective comfort was shown to be improved while saving energy in twelve out of fifteen cases. There is one excep-tional case for both control strategy-2 and control strategy-3, where the supplied airflow rate was increased. Although the priority was given to achieve the highest collective comfort probability in our calculations, the proposed strategies have offered considerable improvements in terms of energy efficiency. Further calculations for revealing achievable energy saving potentials by changing the supplied airflow rates were presented in section 4.4.
