Considering these calculations and the illustrated changes in energy use (i.e., the supplied airflow rate) in Table 4, the best performing strategy (control strategy-3) offered savings with a rate of 35% fan en-ergy and 23% cooling energy in 80% of simulated scenarios with two occupants. This result confirmed that valuable energy could be wasted if HVAC systems are operated in a static manner with full occupancy assumption. Once the number of occupants decreases in an indoor environment, the load of conditioning system could be reactively low-ered by adjusting the relevant parameters. On the other hand, in the maximum occupancy scenario, improving collective comfort probability led to an increase in fan energy by 44% and cooling coil energy by 23%. However, it should be noted that the proposed control strategies were designated to maximize the collective comfort and the energy saving was not prioritized, which could have an influence on this outcome.
4.5. Practical reflections
Lately, with the impact of Covid-19 pandemic, flexible working and hot-desking have become popular space usage strategies for many of-fices. Instead of providing permanent offices, occupants are dynamically allocated to the workstations with the purpose of reducing operational costs, minimizing energy use and promoting work efficiency [56,57]. This growing trend has the potential to both necessitate and enable adopting new operational strategies in buildings for maintaining com-fort requirements and efficient use of resources. In this research, it was demonstrated that personal differences between individuals’ comfort needs and spatial variations in thermal conditions can be leveraged in shared environments to meet the incurring operational needs.
In a practical application, occupants can be optimally assigned to workstations in co-working environments at the start of each day, based on their thermal comfort profiles. This would require accounting for the spatial heterogeneity by having sensors at micro-locations, or employing a type of real-time thermal scanning tool, which will help to have more granular data from the indoor space and consider the environmental conditions in the vicinity of occupants. Accordingly, once occupants arrive to the room, control system can route them to the optimum lo-cations with the help of an intermediary (smartphone application, wearable notification etc.) by processing multiple variables including individuals’ profiles, environmental parameters, occupancy condition for the day, thermal distribution characteristics and so on. The supplied airflow rate and direction can be adjusted at the diffuser level to maintain a particular thermal asymmetry implied by the differences in occupants’ comfort preferences. In doing so, active diffusers (introduced by Jazizadeh et al. [26] for directional airflow) that provide flexibility of airflow adjustments could be employed to achieve the required tem-perature distribution throughout the shared indoor space. For the pro-posed strategies, the establishment of personal comfort profiles needs to be ahead of time and dynamically fed into building control system. In recent years, many researchers have developed personal comfort modeling strategies using various tools including physical human-machine interface [58], smartphones [39], and wearables [59]. The time required for developing personal comfort profiles depends on employed tools, modeling types, and training methods. Although default profiles can be generated with a few data points initially [38], complete and converged personal comfort profiles are usually achieved with a few weeks of data collection procedure [17,59,60]. With the advancing technology, tools used for collecting feedback from the occupants may transform into new forms. The authors anticipate that thermal comfort profiles can be stored in personal devices in near future like a comfort fingerprint, readily available to be fed into the control system in any indoor space. According to the results of this research, in such a scenario, even without the optimal allocation component, the control actions leveraging personal comfort profiles and spatial heterogeneity could provide considerable improvements in creating collectively acceptable conditions in shared environments.
5. Conclusion
This study aims to reveal the potential of accounting for differences in personal comfort preferences and nonuniformity of micro-thermal conditions to improve collective comfort in multi-occupancy spaces. In doing so, an office space with six occupants was adopted as a case study, and unique comfort profiles were assigned to each individual within the defined environment. Thermal distribution characteristics of the space were investigated using CFD simulations under varying supply airflow rates, supply airflow direction, and occupancy settings. We have pro-posed three control strategies with an incremental complexity to delve into the potentials of adjustability in (1) supply airflow direction, (2) supply airflow direction and supply airflow rate, and (3) supply airflow direction, supply airflow rate and occupant locations. We examined collective comfort probabilities under three control strategies at the
